3.3   Tobit Model
          微




   The Tobit model accounts for censorship by predicting a latent variable, y ∗ , instead of directly predicting the
observed variable y with the given predictors and previous data. The values y ∗ and y are related as follows:
                                                 (
                                                   0 if y ∗ ≤ 0,
                                             y=
                                                   y ∗ if y ∗ > 0.

The Tobit model attempts to predict the uncensored data itself given only censored data. This fits the framework
of Olympic medal counts since, as discussed, zero medal counts can be thought of as censored data. The Tobit
model assumes this relationship can be captured from the predictors linearly with the equation

                                                  y ∗ = Xβ + ϵ

Where X is a matrix with the predictor data and β is a column vector of coefficients (so Xβ is some linear
combination of the predictor variables) and ϵ is an error term which is normally distributed with variance σ 2 .
Team 2522820                                                                                                   Page 6


This is usually written as ϵ ∼ N (0, σ 2 ). Since our data is limited, we expect that there are discrepancies between
what they can predict given the complicated nature of why countries win olympic medals. Generally, ϵ is usually
used as a ’noise’ term, but in our implementation we extend this to account for unobserved random effects. To
do this, we have both σn (noise) and σu (unobserved effects) that contribute to the variance of ϵ as follows:

                                                   σ 2 = σn2 + σu2 .

This ensures that we can capture unobserved effects from factors that we cannot add into linear predictors. From
here, our implementation of the Tobit model fits coefficients in β based on previous data to be able to predict y ∗
given predictors X.

3.3.1   Maximum Likelihood Estimate (MLE)
    Our implementation of the Tobit Model optimizes fit using the Maximum Likelihood Estimate (MLE),
which is standard for Tobit models since it can account for both observed and unobserved (censored) parts of
the data. Essentially, MLE aims to maximize the likelihood function, which is the probability that y is observed
given values of β and σ. Maximizing the likelihood means the fitted coefficients for β and σ yield the largest
probability of the actual observed data being observed, which allows us to accurately predict y ∗ given only
predictors X.
                                             L(β, σ) = P (y|β, σ)
Across many observations (yi ) this becomes
                                                                           n
                                                                           Y
                                L(β, σ) = P (y1 , y2 , . . . yn |β, σ) =           P (yi |β, σ)
                                                                           i=1

The likelihood function can then be split up into values where yi = 0 (censored) and yi > 0 (uncensored). The
Tobit model generally assumes y ∗ is normally distributed, which is a restricting assumption but can be altered
if necessary. When yi = 0, we want to compute P (yi = 0|β, σ) = P (y ∗ ≤ 0|β, σ), which can be found with
the normal cumulative density function (CDF), Φ. When yi > 0, we just want P (y ∗ = yi |β, σ), which can be
found with the normal probability density function (PDF), ϕ. Mathematically this becomes
                                                          
                                 ∗                   −Xi β
                             P (y ≤ 0|β, σ) = Φ
                                                        σ
                                                              
                                ∗                    yi − Xi β
                            P (y = yi |β, σ) = ϕ
                                                         σ
                               n                                                  
                              Y                  Y        −Xi β Y          yi − Xi β
                         =⇒       P (yi |β, σ) =      Φ               ϕ                .
                                                           σ                   σ
                               i=1                 yi =0                   yi >0

Aiming to maximize, it helps to take the logarithm of the likelihood function. This helps the function in terms
of differentiability (to be maximized), and whatever maximum is achieved for the log of the likelihood function
will correspond to a maximum of the likelihood function itself.
                                                                X                       
                                          X           yi − Xi β                      0 − Xi β
              ℓ(β, σ) = log(L(β, σ)) =       log ϕ                  +      log Φ
                                                          σ                             σ
                                           yi >0                                   yi =0

Maximizing this (using various numerical methods) give us fitted parameters β and σ for the Tobit model.




